Z||000000||phreakboy4||03-26-2001||07:26 PM||||My 84 fiero keeps going through starters, I think it is due to heat but I am not sure Has anyone dealt with a similar problem||||reg||1
Z||000001||johnt671||03-26-2001||08:29 PM||||When I got my 84 four years ago, the starter that was on it lasted me 6 months.  I replaced it with a parts america starter that fried the solenoid and was falling aprt when i removed it.  Replaced it wiht an Auto Zone starter and saw a much better looking part that the old one.  It's been on there since and just today I had to fix the stud on the solenoid and wrapped it in a heat shield I ordered.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||03-26-2001||08:42 PM||||My '85 2.5 ate two or three starters because Pep Boys did not shim the starter to get correct clearance between starter pinion and flywheel.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||phreakboy4||03-26-2001||09:08 PM||||well I know that when I bought the car about 8 months ago the man told me that he had just put a third starter on it and they were from different places and then about four monthes after i had the car i had to put another one on it and now four months later the solenoid is acting up again||||reg||5
Z||000004||c m west||03-26-2001||10:01 PM||||2 causes <P>either to much or too little shim spacing<BR>and too much heat - or combination of the two||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||03-27-2001||12:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by phreakboy4:<BR><B>My 84 fiero keeps going through starters, I think it is due to heat but I am not sure Has anyone dealt with a similar problem</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Take a soup can and make a heat shield for the solenoid...<BR>Paint it nice!!!<BR>My starter is 2 years old now...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||03-27-2001||01:31 PM||||My '72 Trans Am used to eat starters due to heat. One fix though, was to run a remote starter switch directly from the battery to the starter.  The heat soak makes the starter draw more current than the regular circuit could supply.  So with the factory heat shield and my remote starter button, I never had any more starter problems.  <P>BTW, if you are installing an interior switch, put a relay under the hood.  You don't want starter current snaking through your dashboard and back!||||reg||1
